00:05:47.056 --> 00:05:48.520
So I'm going to talk about the,

00:05:48.521 --> 00:05:50.030
the a Hausdorff distance.

00:05:50.031 --> 00:05:52.135
I'm going to find
the Hausdorff distance first.

00:05:52.136 --> 00:05:56.110
So if X and Y are compact sets
in a metric space,

00:05:56.111 --> 00:06:00.185
then you can define
the distance from a point here,

00:06:00.186 --> 00:06:02.570
to the point in X
that's set in Y,

00:06:02.571 --> 00:06:04.660
as the distance to the point

00:06:04.661 --> 00:06:07.690
that minimizes
the distance in the outset.

00:06:07.691 --> 00:06:11.905
And then you can define
the distance between X and Y,

00:06:11.906 --> 00:06:15.630
as you take among all the
distances from points to Y,

00:06:15.631 --> 00:06:19.550
you take the infimum,
sorry the supremum.

00:06:19.551 --> 00:06:24.580
And then, since this definition

00:06:24.581 --> 00:06:27.360
is not symmetric between
X and Y and Y and X,

00:06:27.361 --> 00:06:29.195
then you define
the Hausdorff distance as

00:06:29.196 --> 00:06:33.720
the max between
d(X,Y) and d(Y, X).

00:06:33.730 --> 00:06:38.190
And, so this is
the distance between

00:06:38.191 --> 00:06:41.650
two sets that are embedded
in the same metric space.

00:06:41.651 --> 00:06:44.055
They are compact sets
in the metric space.

00:06:44.056 --> 00:06:47.630
So the Gromov-Haudsorff
distance is defined for

00:06:47.631 --> 00:06:49.640
two compact metric
spaces that are

00:06:49.641 --> 00:06:52.205
not necessarily embedded
in the same space.

00:06:52.206 --> 00:06:54.605
The way it is defined is if

00:06:54.606 --> 00:06:57.095
X and Y are
compact metric spaces,

00:06:57.096 --> 00:07:00.080
then you consider
all isometric embeddings,

00:07:00.081 --> 00:07:02.740
from X to Z and from Y to Z,

00:07:02.741 --> 00:07:04.480
to a larger space Z.

00:07:04.481 --> 00:07:09.470
And then, you define
the distance between X and

00:07:09.471 --> 00:07:14.925
Y as the Hausdorff distance
between F of X,

00:07:14.926 --> 00:07:16.890
the embedding of X and
the embedding of Y.

00:07:16.891 --> 00:07:18.730
And then you take
the infimum among

00:07:18.731 --> 00:07:21.415
all possible
isometric embeddings.

00:07:21.416 --> 00:07:26.690
And this looks hard to compute.

00:07:26.691 --> 00:07:28.990
But then we'll see
how to compute it,

00:07:28.991 --> 00:07:31.610
and then fact is

00:07:31.611 --> 00:07:33.320
that the
Gromov-Haudsorff distance

00:07:33.321 --> 00:07:34.955
between two metric
spaces is zero,

00:07:34.956 --> 00:07:39.384
even a leaf, this pen
is isometric.

00:07:39.385 --> 00:07:46.280
This is the natural distance
between metric spaces.

00:07:46.281 --> 00:07:48.290
It has interesting results and

00:07:48.291 --> 00:07:50.885
applications in
Riemannian Geometry,

00:07:50.886 --> 00:07:55.200
and it has been introduced
by Gromov in order to prove

00:07:55.201 --> 00:07:56.925
the famous Gromov theorem

00:07:56.926 --> 00:08:01.140
on growth like groups
of polynomial growth.

00:08:01.141 --> 00:08:04.110
That says that, if you have
a final generator group,

00:08:04.111 --> 00:08:06.840
and then you take
a number of generators.

00:08:06.841 --> 00:08:10.120
And then, you see
how many elements

00:08:10.121 --> 00:08:11.240
of the group you can write as

00:08:11.241 --> 00:08:12.795
a combination of
these generators.

00:08:12.796 --> 00:08:14.215
And if that grows

00:08:14.216 --> 00:08:16.705
polynomially in
the number of generators,

00:08:16.706 --> 00:08:19.250
then it means that your group
has a large part that

00:08:19.251 --> 00:08:23.880
is Abelia, like commute.

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:30.820
So, in our case,

00:08:30.821 --> 00:08:31.965
we're going to I think of

00:08:31.966 --> 00:08:34.525
Gromov-Hausdorff distance
in point clouds.

00:08:34.526 --> 00:08:39.030
And one motivation to think of

00:08:39.031 --> 00:08:41.190
all these point clouds
is because if you

00:08:41.191 --> 00:08:44.570
have X and Y on they are
compact matrix spaces,

00:08:44.571 --> 00:08:46.925
then you can consider
extra epsilon-nets,

00:08:46.926 --> 00:08:49.005
which is subsets where

00:08:49.006 --> 00:08:50.970
every point in
your manifold is within

00:08:50.971 --> 00:08:54.720
epsilon of one point in
your epsilon-net, at least.

00:08:54.721 --> 00:08:56.970
And then, if you

00:08:56.971 --> 00:09:00.345
take epsilon-net of X
and epsilon-net of Y,

00:09:00.346 --> 00:09:02.530
and they you take
epsilon ten to zero,

00:09:02.531 --> 00:09:05.860
then the distance
between X and Y is

00:09:05.861 --> 00:09:07.240
the limit with epsilon ten to

00:09:07.241 --> 00:09:09.335
zero of the distance
between the epsilon-nets.

00:09:09.336 --> 00:09:12.030
So, it's a good approximation to

00:09:12.031 --> 00:09:14.830
computing in point
close to actually,

00:09:14.831 --> 00:09:17.080
computed in the
whole metric space.

00:09:17.081 --> 00:09:20.115
And you can use this to

00:09:20.116 --> 00:09:23.420
compare geometric objects,
like surfaces.

00:09:23.421 --> 00:09:25.360
But also, for example,

00:09:25.361 --> 00:09:29.620
this is a paper in 2011 in

00:09:29.621 --> 00:09:34.150
PNAS by speaker Daubachies
and collaborators,

00:09:34.151 --> 00:09:38.470
