In the first graph on J prime,

00:29:49.136 --> 00:29:52.780
S prime. Very sorry.

00:29:52.781 --> 00:29:55.020
In the second graph
and you put out

00:29:55.021 --> 00:29:56.940
one if you are in this case,

00:29:56.941 --> 00:30:01.915
and you put a zero if you
are in a different case.

00:30:01.916 --> 00:30:05.860
So basically you have an edge
here and an edge here,

00:30:05.861 --> 00:30:08.475
you put on one and
then zero otherwise.

00:30:08.476 --> 00:30:13.130
The algorithm, just
a simple thing,

00:30:13.131 --> 00:30:15.630
you take the top eigenvector of

00:30:15.631 --> 00:30:18.490
Gamma and you round
it to a permutation.

00:30:18.491 --> 00:30:24.580
&gt;&gt; How do you do the rounding?

00:30:24.581 --> 00:30:27.360
&gt;&gt; Yes, that's
a very good question.

00:30:27.361 --> 00:30:29.860
So something that you

00:30:29.861 --> 00:30:32.955
can do is you can solve
a linear problem and

00:30:32.956 --> 00:30:44.795
you maximize it with
a Birkhoff polytope.

00:30:44.796 --> 00:30:47.465
So given this vector,

00:30:47.466 --> 00:30:54.205
you write it as a matrix
like a square matrix

00:30:54.206 --> 00:30:57.415
and then you take
the maximum of that,

00:30:57.416 --> 00:30:59.540
times X such that X is

00:30:59.541 --> 00:31:03.490
bistochastic and then you
know that the solution

00:31:03.491 --> 00:31:05.720
of that linear problem
is going to

00:31:05.721 --> 00:31:10.675
be integer because
all the vertices are goal.

00:31:10.676 --> 00:31:14.155
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] could
you repeat the process?

00:31:14.156 --> 00:31:15.705
&gt;&gt; So what you do is you have

00:31:15.706 --> 00:31:18.950
these top eigenvectors you
write it as a matrix and then

00:31:18.951 --> 00:31:21.905
you consider the max
of that vector

00:31:21.906 --> 00:31:26.880
in a [inaudible] with X
such that X is bistochastic.

00:31:26.881 --> 00:31:28.720
&gt;&gt; X is matrix.

00:31:28.721 --> 00:31:30.720
&gt;&gt; A matrix, that is correct.

00:31:30.721 --> 00:31:34.110
&gt;&gt; But it's still fractional,

00:31:34.111 --> 00:31:36.180
right? Whatever you [inaudible].

00:31:36.181 --> 00:31:41.490
&gt;&gt; No, because all-

00:31:41.491 --> 00:31:42.550
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah sure [inaudible].

00:31:42.551 --> 00:31:45.750
&gt;&gt; So these algorithms will work

00:31:45.751 --> 00:31:49.040
in a very, very few instances.

00:31:49.041 --> 00:31:59.285
For example, if Gamma is
not the top eigenvector,

00:31:59.286 --> 00:32:01.855
it's not simple,
like there's more

00:32:01.856 --> 00:32:03.790
than one eigenvector with

00:32:03.791 --> 00:32:07.985
the same eigenvalue, then
it's not going to work.

00:32:07.986 --> 00:32:12.100
And if you have a lot of

00:32:12.101 --> 00:32:15.600
noise then it's also
not going to work.

00:32:15.601 --> 00:32:17.265
But that's like a base line.

00:32:17.266 --> 00:32:21.220
So basically what we want
to do is we want to modify

00:32:21.221 --> 00:32:24.555
this algorithm to see

00:32:24.556 --> 00:32:27.080
if we can make it work
in a more general case.

00:32:27.081 --> 00:32:31.735
So this comes from
the idea that,

00:32:31.736 --> 00:32:34.010
for example, when you
want to go clustering,

00:32:34.011 --> 00:32:35.890
you can consider
spectral clustering.

00:32:35.891 --> 00:32:37.560
You take, let's say,

00:32:37.561 --> 00:32:38.900
that you want to
find two clusters to

00:32:38.901 --> 00:32:40.975
take a top eigenvector of

00:32:40.976 --> 00:32:45.510
a distance matrix or
Laplacian and then and

00:32:45.511 --> 00:32:51.015
then you see
this top eigenvector

00:32:51.016 --> 00:32:53.940
works in a very good case

00:32:53.941 --> 00:32:55.475
where the clusters
are separated,

00:32:55.476 --> 00:32:58.490
but sometimes you
can actually modify.

00:32:58.491 --> 00:33:01.790
While the when you take
the top eigenvector and

00:33:01.791 --> 00:33:05.940
then you get the clustering
works better in that case.

00:33:05.941 --> 00:33:10.090
And this is work that is
very precise and is work by

00:33:10.091 --> 00:33:12.095
Emmanuel Abbe and
Andrea Montanari

00:33:12.096 --> 00:33:14.335
and they study
algorithms of these form.

00:33:14.336 --> 00:33:16.140
Like what is
the best spectrum method

00:33:16.141 --> 00:33:17.680
for this clustering problem.

00:33:17.681 --> 00:33:21.325
So this is basically
what we want to do here.

00:33:21.326 --> 00:33:25.110
Like, look for
a good spectrum method

00:33:25.111 --> 00:33:28.265
in this quadratic
assignment problem.

00:33:28.266 --> 00:33:30.845
So we're going to consider

00:33:30.846 --> 00:33:35.760
projected power iteration
where one can think of the..

00:33:35.761 --> 00:33:37.885
&gt;&gt; In this equation,

00:33:37.886 --> 00:33:42.165
what's the problem
we're solving?

00:33:42.166 --> 00:33:47.225
&gt;&gt; We're going to find
the matching A and B,

00:33:47.226 --> 00:33:50.170
two graphs and we
want to find matching.

00:33:50.171 --> 00:33:53.580
&gt;&gt; So we match, so it's like
we want permutation on one

00:33:53.581 --> 00:33:55.620
to one type of

00:33:55.621 --> 00:33:58.465
vertices so that what, what's
the objective function?

00:33:58.466 --> 00:34:00.310
&gt;&gt; The same as before. Right?

00:34:00.311 --> 00:34:02.075
&gt;&gt; Graph isomorphism?

00:34:02.076 --> 00:34:03.645
&gt;&gt; Sorry?

00:34:03.646 --> 00:34:05.280
&gt;&gt; Graph isomorphism?

00:34:05.281 --> 00:34:06.430
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:34:06.431 --> 00:34:10.090
&gt;&gt; I see. So we get one
for every age that got

00:34:10.091 --> 00:34:14.305
matched otherwise zero
in all three cases. Okay.

00:34:14.306 --> 00:34:15.815
&gt;&gt; So.

00:34:15.816 --> 00:34:18.275
&gt;&gt; So number of common ages.

00:34:18.276 --> 00:34:21.775
&gt;&gt; So you want to,

00:34:21.776 --> 00:34:29.385
maximize the trace of
AX BX transpose and X.

00:34:29.386 --> 00:34:31.800
&gt;&gt; Okay, so we're solving

00:34:31.801 --> 00:34:33.760
genome problem but
for the case where A

00:34:33.761 --> 00:34:35.755
and B are 01 matrices.

00:34:35.756 --> 00:34:36.071
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:34:36.071 --> 00:34:38.530
&gt;&gt; And they are
symmetric on levels?

00:34:38.531 --> 00:34:40.115
&gt;&gt; Symmetric, yeah.

00:34:40.116 --> 00:34:43.990
&gt;&gt; Okay, okay.

00:34:48.350 --> 00:34:51.920
&gt;&gt; So we're going to go and see

00:34:51.921 --> 00:34:54.120
a projected power iteration.

00:34:54.121 --> 00:34:58.460
And the idea is that when
you take the top eigenvector,

00:34:58.461 --> 00:35:01.720
you can do it using
a power method.

00:35:01.721 --> 00:35:03.500
You start with a vector and then

00:35:03.501 --> 00:35:05.505
you multiply the matrix
times the vector,

00:35:05.506 --> 00:35:07.410
and you multiply that times

00:35:07.411 --> 00:35:11.565
the matrix again and
again until convergence.

00:35:11.566 --> 00:35:14.590
The projected power iteration,

00:35:14.591 --> 00:35:17.190
what it does is it adds

00:35:17.191 --> 00:35:19.625
thresholding step after

00:35:19.626 --> 00:35:22.625
each iteration of
the power method.

00:35:22.626 --> 00:35:24.100
So instead of multiplying

00:35:24.101 --> 00:35:28.345
matrix times
matrix times vector,

00:35:28.346 --> 00:35:32.590
you do matrix times vector
and then your threshold that,

00:35:32.591 --> 00:35:34.290
and then you multiply it by

00:35:34.291 --> 00:35:36.005
matrix again, and
then your threshold that.

00:35:36.006 --> 00:35:41.970
So you do a thresholding
after each step.

00:35:41.971 --> 00:35:46.085
So the thresholding
can be a rounding

00:35:46.086 --> 00:35:50.660
to the set like a complete
projection to your set,

00:35:50.661 --> 00:35:53.870
or you choose
a close point in your set,

00:35:53.871 --> 00:36:00.995
or just some thresholding
or some softening,

00:36:00.996 --> 00:36:06.200
but not necessarily going
to the set of permutations.

00:36:07.900 --> 00:36:13.835
Something that you
can also do is,

00:36:13.836 --> 00:36:15.650
here you know that,

00:36:15.651 --> 00:36:18.060
the multiplication
of Gamma times v,

00:36:18.061 --> 00:36:19.550
and if you look
at the position t,

00:36:19.551 --> 00:36:21.595
then you get
this multiplication of

00:36:21.596 --> 00:36:24.685
the matrix times the vector
is going to be l,

00:36:24.686 --> 00:36:27.970
and it's the same
for all the entries.

00:36:27.971 --> 00:36:32.385
So there is a way you can,

00:36:32.386 --> 00:36:34.110
something that you
may want to do is

00:36:34.111 --> 00:36:36.155
to avoid the backtracking terms,

00:36:36.156 --> 00:36:41.610
meaning that you can think
of what the position,

00:36:41.611 --> 00:36:45.730
what vl has in this L position

00:36:45.731 --> 00:36:49.745
is going to affect what
Gamma v has in the position T.

00:36:49.746 --> 00:36:51.610
And you don't- you want to avoid

00:36:51.611 --> 00:36:53.420
backtracking in terms
meaning that you

00:36:53.421 --> 00:36:57.620
don't want that what L

00:36:57.621 --> 00:37:00.685
had in the previous step

00:37:00.686 --> 00:37:03.170
affecting what l
has in future step.

00:37:03.171 --> 00:37:06.740
It's just like some
regularization that you can do.

00:37:06.741 --> 00:37:12.410
And this regularization
is used for this,

00:37:12.411 --> 00:37:15.280
what they call approximate
message passing algorithms.

00:37:15.281 --> 00:37:21.740
And in fact if you want
to remove this thing,

00:37:21.741 --> 00:37:27.740
you can write it out as

00:37:27.741 --> 00:37:35.055
the regular multiplication of

00:37:35.056 --> 00:37:38.185
the matrix plus
some correction term

00:37:38.186 --> 00:37:41.385
that comes from removing
the backtracking terms,

00:37:41.386 --> 00:37:42.780
and this is called
the outsider term

00:37:42.781 --> 00:37:45.445
in approximate message
passing algorithms.

00:37:45.446 --> 00:37:50.335
But you can't compute
it explicitly.

00:37:50.336 --> 00:37:55.750
Basically, this is
an ongoing project.

00:37:55.751 --> 00:37:59.855
Basically, what we
do is just realize

00:37:59.856 --> 00:38:05.805
that this projected
power iteration actually

00:38:05.806 --> 00:38:14.220
can recover the alignment

00:38:14.221 --> 00:38:18.635
between the graphs in
